            "decimals": 2,
            "label": "DNSMOS background noise",
            "note": "Noise is the hardest thing on this page to measure, so we use DNSMOS BAK, a neural network from Microsoft. It predicts how a listener would rate the background, on ITU-T P.835’s scale of 1 to 5.\n\nP.835 puts words on the numbers. At 3 you notice the background but it does not get in the way. At 4 you barely notice it. At 5 you do not notice it at all. Every row on the ladder carries a 10 second clip from a real recording at that score, so you can hear each one for yourself.\n\nBAK reads a 9.01 second window, the only length it is calibrated for. We slide that window every 3 seconds and take each track’s median. We drop windows under 25 percent speech, because the model only hears noise behind a voice.",
            "p5": 3.49,
            "p50": 3.97,
            "p95": 4.14,
            "unit": "BAK"
          }
        ],
